## Why This Is Asked
This question probes end-to-end CV system design under edge constraints, occlusion and quick adaptation to new tools. It tests architectural choices, data strategy, and robust evaluation.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular pose estimation with depth priors and CAD priors
- Edge latency optimization and 40 ms budget
- 4-shot on-device adaptation; domain randomization + weak supervision

## Code Example
```javascript
// sketch: minimal inference pipeline outline
function infer(frame) {
  const boxes = detect2D(frame);
  const pose = estimate6DoF(frame, boxes);
  return pose;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle tool reconfiguration with new geometries in production?
- What failure modes are most critical and how would you monitor them?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design production-ready, on-device face verification systems with robust anti-spoofing capabilities under strict computational constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Edge-optimized inference architectures
- 3D head pose estimation from facial landmarks
- Temporal liveness detection for spoof resistance
- Parameter-efficient 5-shot adaptation via adapter layers
- Hybrid synthetic-to-real data generation strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: unified inference pipeline
function processFrame(frame) {
  const backbone = extractFeatures(frame);
  const detection = faceDetectionHead(backbone);
  const liveness = temporalLivenessHead(backbone);
  return { detection, liveness };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes on-device real-time hazard detection and 3D distance estimation under unseen categories, reflecting safety-critical constraints common in field deployments.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time monocular depth on edge
- On-device meta-learning (5-shot)
- Synthetic+real data with domain randomization
- Temporal tracking and occlusion robustness
- Evaluation: detection, depth error, latency

## Code Example

```javascript
function forward(img, backbone, detectorHead, depthHead) {
  const feats = backbone.extract(img);
  const boxes = detectorHead.predict(feats);
  const depth = depthHead.predict(feats);
  return { boxes, depth };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you ablate depth head accuracy vs. detector latency?
- What failure modes dominate in cluttered sites, and how would you mitigate them?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a compact computer vision pipeline with practical on-device adaptation under tight latency constraints. It also probes understanding of data strategy and evaluation beyond single-object pose tasks.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight detection with 2D bounding boxes
- Depth proxy estimation from object size
- Simple tracking with temporal consistency
- On-device few-shot adaptation (3-shot)
- Counting metrics and evaluation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: detection + tracking update loop
for (const frame of video) {
  const detections = detector.predict(frame);
  const depthProxy = estimateDepth(detections, calibration);
  const tracked = tracker.update(detections, depthProxy);
  yield { boxes: tracked, count: countPackages(tracked) };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests few-shot, on-device adaptation, and robust pose estimation under occlusion and motion.

## Key Concepts
- Few-shot on-device adaptation; - monocular 6-DoF pose; - temporal consistency; - synthetic-to-real data.

## Code Example
```javascript
function weightedLoss(posePred, poseGT, maskPred, maskGT){
  const Lp = l2(posePred, poseGT);
  const Lm = crossEntropy(maskPred, maskGT);
  return Lp + Lm;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to more than 3-shot adaptation with minimal latency?
- How would you evaluate failure modes under strong motion blur and fabric reflectivity?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design an edge-friendly, few-shot adaptable vision system with robust localization under real-time constraints and production-domain data shifts.

## Key Concepts

- Edge-efficient architectures, few-shot adapters
- Synthetic+real data for defect distribution
- Temporal consistency and localization losses

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: forward pass outline
function forward(frame, adapter) {
  const feat = backbone(frame);
  const heatmap = detectorHead(feat);
  const adapted = adapter(feat);
  const localized = fuse(heatmap, adapted);
  return localized;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate real-time performance on hardware with varying thermal conditions?
- What failure modes require monitoring in production and how would you mitigate them?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to combine learned features with classical cues under tight on-device latency, in a realistic retail robotics setting.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight CNN + classical feature fusion
- On-device few-shot adaptation (3-5 shots)
- Latency budgeting (<=50 ms/frame)
- Robust evaluation: per-item accuracy, misorientation metrics, throughput

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code sketch of forward pass
function forward(frame) {
  const rois = detectROIs(frame);
  for (const roi of rois) {
    const cnnFeat = smallCNN(roi);
    const cues = extractCues(roi); // hist, edges, texture
    const fused = fuse(cnnFeat, cues);
    const label = orientationHead(fused);
    if (label === 'misoriented') alert(roi);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle label drift with new item designs?
- What ablation studies would you run to verify cue contributions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to handle equipment with strong reflective/transparent surfaces, a realistic industrial setting, and on-device adaptation under tight latency budgets.

## Key Concepts
- Reflective/transparent object pose estimation
- Differentiable rendering for photometric consistency
- On-device 3-shot adaptation via compact adapters
- Synthetic+real data with material randomization
- Edge latency and robustness under glare

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode sketch
function forward(frame) {
  const feats = backbone(frame);
  const seg = segHead(feats);
  const pose = poseHead(feats);
  const render = differentiableRenderer(pose, frame);
  return {seg, pose, loss: computeLoss(render, frame)};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to varying refractive indices?
- How would you quantify uncertainty in pose under specular highlights?

